WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.891
When legacy native Java deserialization
cannot be removed immediately,

2
00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:05.447
filtering is damage reduction.

3
00:00:05.527 --> 00:00:09.137
Java serialization filters, including
ObjectInputFilter-style controls,

4
00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:10.912
can help restrict incoming streams.

5
00:00:10.992 --> 00:00:14.000
Useful controls may include allowed
classes, rejected classes,

6
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.416
maximum depth, maximum array size, maximum
references, maximum bytes,

7
00:00:17.496 --> 00:00:20.395
and other resource limits supported by the
runtime and design.

8
00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:23.210
The filter should make the expected object
graph explicit.

9
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.040
Filters should be context-specific.

10
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.893
One broad global permission to deserialize
many classes is much less useful than a

11
00:00:28.973 --> 00:00:30.833
narrow filter tied to a single workflow.

12
00:00:30.913 --> 00:00:34.358
A class allowlist should answer which
exact classes are valid here and why.

13
00:00:34.438 --> 00:00:38.321
Reject-by-default behavior is safer than
accepting undecided or unknown classes.

14
00:00:38.401 --> 00:00:40.862
If a stream contains a class outside the
expected set,

15
00:00:40.942 --> 00:00:44.606
the safest response is usually to reject
the input and record a safe operational

16
00:00:44.686 --> 00:00:45.069
signal.

17
00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:48.212
Filters reduce risk, but they do not make
risky designs fully safe.

18
00:00:48.292 --> 00:00:51.519
Deserialization inputs still need
authentication, authorization,

19
00:00:51.599 --> 00:00:54.060
size limits, monitoring, and safe failure
behavior.

20
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.273
If a file import, session restore, cache
read,

21
00:00:56.353 --> 00:00:59.743
or message consumer uses object streams,
that path should be documented,

22
00:00:59.823 --> 00:01:02.613
tested, owned, and included in incident
response planning.

23
00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:06.357
Filter configuration also needs review
when dependencies, runtime versions,

24
00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:07.859
or serialized classes change.

25
00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:11.658
Treat filtering as migration support
rather than a reason to keep object streams

26
00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:12.175
forever.

27
00:01:12.255 --> 00:01:14.990
A good near-term plan narrows classes and
resource limits.

28
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.062
A better long-term plan replaces untrusted
object deserialization with constrained

29
00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:21.549
data formats, DTOs, schemas, and explicit
mapping.

30
00:01:21.629 --> 00:01:24.472
Teams should know which legacy paths
remain, why they remain,

31
00:01:24.552 --> 00:01:26.740
and what must happen before they can be
retired.
